5,1,1,talk,Toshio,,"To ensure we make an objective choice, we should examine the actual utility of past projects. I suggest we look into the post-occupancy review ratings and the survey findings in the older blocks.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
6,1,1,talk,Victoria,,"I agree with Toshio. To complement that, I believe we need to understand the operational impact on our scheduling. I suggest looking into the timetable records on rooms out of service and the booking data.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
7,1,1,talk,George,,I believe we must also consider the human element. I would like to examine the staffing posts since the hiring freeze and the bench technicians' weekly manual routines to see where the real pressure lies.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
8,1,1,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree that we need a comprehensive view. To address potential financial and timing risks, I want to look into the tender records over three budget cycles and the decade of delivery schedules by project rationale.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
9,1,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are all eager for data. To ensure our budget isn't merely a work of fiction, I shall examine the tender records over three budget cycles and the renewal reserve for equipment.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
10,1,2,talk,Toshio,,"It seems we have a broad range of data points. @Victoria, your focus on room availability will be a useful counterpoint to the user satisfaction ratings I am seeking. I believe this approach covers our bases.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
11,1,2,talk,Victoria,,"Thank you, Toshio. I believe that by comparing the booking data with the rooms out of service, we can identify whether our current space allocation truly meets the actual demand or if we are facing systemic shortages.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
12,1,2,talk,George,,"While space allocation is important, we must not forget the people maintaining those spaces. If we ignore the operational burden on our staff, any new facility will only add to their existing strain.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
13,1,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, your point is valid. However, we must also ensure that the projects we approve are actually deliverable within the timeframe. I am concerned that unrealistic schedules often lead to rushed, poor-quality results.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
14,1,2,talk,Benjamin,,"A fair point, Mizuki. However, a schedule is only as reliable as the budget supporting it. I suspect we may find a charming discrepancy between what was promised on paper and what the market actually demanded.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
15,1,3,talk,Toshio,,"The tension between budget and delivery is clear. However, if we ignore whether previous projects actually served their purpose, we risk repeating the same mistakes regardless of the budget. We must prioritize actual utility.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
16,1,3,talk,Victoria,,"Utility is essential, but we must also consider the cost of the transition. If we don't account for the loss of usable space during construction, the immediate operational cost might outweigh the long-term utility.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
17,1,3,talk,George,,"I understand the concern for transition costs, but we must be careful not to prioritize the building over the people. If the staff are already at their limit, any transition will be a struggle.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
18,1,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I agree that staff wellbeing is crucial. However, if we ignore the pattern of scheduling slips, we risk creating a chaotic environment that stresses the staff even more during the construction phase. We need realistic timelines.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
19,1,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It is truly heartwarming to see such passion for the theoretical. However, let us not forget that a project's ""utility"" is often a luxury afforded only to those who can actually afford to finish the building.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
20,1,4,talk,Toshio,,"Regardless of the financial constraints Benjamin mentions, we cannot afford to build something that fails its users. Let us proceed with these data points to ensure our final choice is grounded in reality.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
21,1,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. By analyzing both the long-term utility and the immediate operational disruptions, we can make a decision that is sustainable for the university's daily functions. I look forward to seeing what the data reveals.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
22,1,4,talk,George,,I agree. Let us see if the data reflects the reality of those working on the ground. I hope we can find a solution that provides genuine relief to our exhausted staff.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
23,1,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree. By combining the operational data with a critical look at our delivery history, we can avoid the pitfalls of over-optimism and select a plan that is truly feasible. I look forward to the results.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
24,1,4,talk,Benjamin,,Indeed. Let us hope the data provides a refreshing dose of reality to our aspirations. I shall be most interested to see if our financial projections have been living in a fantasy world.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
