35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data is revealing. Post-occupancy reviews show that projects benefiting a single wing performed poorly, while open-access spaces were highly rated. We must prioritize plans that serve the widest possible user base to ensure utility.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings are concerning. Booking data shows specialist rooms are often idle, while general-purpose room requests are constantly denied. Furthermore, every room taken out of service requires expensive off-site hired space for two terms.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,"My findings confirm a worrying trend. While some new hires have capacity, our experienced bench technicians are bogged down by tedious manual routines and checks that should be automated. We are wasting human potential on basic upkeep.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"The data confirms my fears. Projects driven by ""comfort"" or ""appeal"" consistently slip by a year or more, while only essential inspection-led works stay on track. We must be wary of any plan based on aspirations rather than necessity.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings suggest our financial planning is a bit of a fairy tale. Mechanical and plant costs have surged by twenty percent, yet we've failed to even set aside reserves for replacing aging AV equipment. We are underestimating costs.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Victoria, your data on general-purpose room shortages aligns perfectly with my findings. If we invest in a project that only benefits a small group, we are ignoring a systemic demand and risking another low-rated outcome.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, exactly. But we must also consider the hidden costs. If we choose a project that takes several general-purpose rooms out of service, the expense of hiring off-site replacements will be significant and disruptive.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,"@Victoria, I understand the cost of off-site space, but we must also consider the cost of burnout. If we don't automate these manual routines, we are essentially paying skilled technicians to be janitors.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I sympathize with the staff, but we must be realistic. If we choose a complex project that slips by a year, as the data suggests ""appeal"" projects do, the burnout will only intensify due to prolonged chaos.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is fascinating how we discuss ""hidden costs"" while ignoring the most obvious one: the market. If we approve a plan based on outdated figures, we aren't just risking a delay; we are inviting a budget collapse.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, budget is a constraint, but not the only metric of success. If we build something cheap that nobody can use effectively, we have wasted the budget entirely. We need a project with broad, cross-departmental utility.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, I agree. However, we must be cautious. If we prioritize a high-utility project that requires extensive room closures, the immediate financial and operational strain of off-site hiring could jeopardize the project's overall viability.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,"@Victoria, the strain is already here. We cannot keep delaying relief for our staff because we fear temporary disruption. A project that automates manual burdens will provide permanent relief and long-term operational efficiency.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, permanent relief is only possible if the project is actually completed. If we choose a plan based on ""appeal"" or ""comfort"" that slips by a year, we prolong the instability and the staff's burden.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, your caution is prudent, but let us be honest: a project that ""slips"" often does so because the budget was a polite suggestion rather than a reality. We cannot afford another financial fantasy.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"We must find a balance. A project that provides broad utility and addresses systemic shortages, while remaining grounded in realistic costs and timelines, is our only viable path. Let us evaluate the candidates with these specific risks in mind.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I agree. We must specifically scrutinize how many general-purpose rooms each candidate removes from the timetable and whether the projected costs include the inevitable expense of off-site replacements during the construction phase.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,George,,"I agree. However, we must also scrutinize which candidates actually reduce the manual workload for our technicians. A project that improves the facility but ignores the staff's daily burden is not a sustainable improvement.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree. We must prioritize candidates that are based on necessity rather than aspiration. If a plan is framed as a ""luxury"" or ""improvement for appeal,"" we must assume it will slip and avoid it to protect our staff and budget.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I concur. Let us be particularly vigilant regarding any plan involving heavy mechanical upgrades or high-end equipment. Given the market surge and our lack of reserves, such ""aspirations"" are merely invitations for a budget deficit.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
