60,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Based on the data, Option B and D offer the broad utility we need. However, Option B is in a ground-floor hall, which my findings suggest may contain hazardous materials that could cause significant delays.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, Option B addresses the general-purpose room shortage, but if it causes delays, the operational cost rises. Option A is concerning because it likely takes multiple teaching labs out of service, necessitating expensive off-site hiring.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"Option A is the only choice that directly addresses the manual burdens I discovered. Replacing the extraction equipment would automate those tedious routines, providing much-needed relief to our exhausted bench technicians.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, while I understand the benefit, Option A involves heavy mechanical and plant work, which Benjamin noted is seeing huge cost surges. Furthermore, it's a complex upgrade that risks the scheduling slips I'm concerned about.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki is quite right. Option A is a financial minefield given the twenty percent surge in plant costs. Option D, meanwhile, relies on AV kit for which we have no renewal reserves. It is a recipe for a deficit.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, if Option D is a financial risk, then Option B is the most logical choice for utility. It serves the widest user base and avoids the mechanical cost surges of Option A.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, Option B is appealing for utility, but we must confirm it doesn't displace existing general-purpose rooms. If it does, the cost of off-site replacements could be substantial. Is the hall truly underused?","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Victoria, the hall's usage is secondary to the crisis in our labs. If we choose Option B, we are simply moving students around while our technicians continue to struggle with outdated, manual systems. We cannot ignore the staff.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I cannot ignore the staff, but I also cannot ignore the data. Option A is a high-risk project. Between the plant cost surges and the complexity, it is the most likely to slip and fail.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@George, your empathy is admirable, but we cannot build a sanctuary for technicians on a foundation of debt. Option A is a fiscal disaster waiting to happen. Option C, however, is remarkably stable.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, Option C provides minimal utility compared to the others. It is a surface-level improvement. We must prioritize a project that actually solves a systemic problem, like the shortage of general-purpose space in Option B.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, I agree that Option C is insufficient. Between the remaining choices, Option B seems the most sustainable, provided the hall is truly underused and doesn't trigger the expensive off-site hiring costs I'm worried about.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"@Victoria, the ""systemic problem"" isn't just room counts; it's the operational collapse of our lab support. If we prioritize a study hall over the people keeping our science facilities running, we are failing our most essential staff.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I understand the urgency, but Option A is a gamble we cannot afford. It's a complex mechanical project—the exact type that risks massive cost overruns and scheduling slips. Option B is a far more stable and feasible choice.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@George, while I appreciate the sentiment, a ""systemic problem"" is not solved by a project that bankrupts the department. Option B is far more fiscally predictable than A or D. Let us be pragmatic.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"We have a consensus on the risks of A and D. While B has the potential for delays due to hazardous materials, its broad utility and fiscal predictability make it the most responsible choice. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. Option B addresses the critical shortage of general-purpose rooms without the extreme financial risks of A or D. As long as the hall is underused, it minimizes operational disruption. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,I cannot in good conscience vote for a project that ignores the people who keep this university running. Our technicians are at a breaking point. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,I must prioritize feasibility. Option A is too risky given the cost surges and the high probability of scheduling slips. Option B provides broad utility while remaining a manageable project. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a pity we cannot automate the budget, but we must choose the path of least financial volatility. Option B is the only rational choice that avoids a fiscal catastrophe. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
